WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:09.929
These two scenes almost 20 years apart, both
showed their digitally created main character

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:12.480
waking up.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.520
They also served as the big reveal of a technical
breakthrough.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.920
Final Fantasy: The Spirits Within was one
of the first movies with a realistic human

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.750
CGI character — at least in theory.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:31.439
To our eyes today, the movement and textures
make it look at best like a video game cutscene.

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:32.800
But focus on the skin.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.730
How did we go from lifeless skin to skin that,
on Alita, a stylized character with giant

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.780
eyes and a robot body, looks so much better?

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.789
How do we make fake skin look real?

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:51.500
That journey to realistic skin includes pore
mapping, the appearance of light on apples

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.842
and chicken, and knowing the difference between
a glass of whole and skim milk.

00:00:57.843 --> 00:01:03.014
I’m finding it really hard not to feel like
a total stoner thinking about my skin — 

00:01:03.139 --> 00:01:05.965
I’m touching my face right now.

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:09.840
“Honestly, when we go through looking at
this stuff, we're all doing this and like

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.479
trying to look in the mirror.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:13.660
My name is Nick Epstein.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.049
I was a visual effects supervisor on Alita:
Battle Angel.”

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:24.390
Alita: Battle Angel is a 2019 action movie
based on the manga and it features a stylized

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.910
character that's perfectly believable.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.600
She's come a long way from this guy, the Scorpion
King from the Mummy Returns.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:38.090
This shot is so infamous that the VFX YouTube
channel Corridor Crew spent a whole video

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.240
trying to fix it.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.899
There are a lot of problems, but a big one
is the skin.

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:46.219
The Mummy Returns’ Scorpion King is played
by the Rock.

00:01:46.219 --> 00:01:47.479
You wouldn't know it.

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:51.159
But Alita definitely resembles the actor who
played her.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:56.619
“We needed to make sure that we were capturing
Rosa’s performance, like the heart of the

00:01:56.619 --> 00:01:58.969
movie was really Rosa’s performance as Alita.

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:03.430
So we actually built a fully digital version
of Rosa.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.789
And then we could apply sort of our sort of
more, I guess, normal realism factors and

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.450
barometers to that.”

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.870
Getting that facial model right is a crucial
first step for the skin’s movement.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.230
When the hard work begins.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.770
“I have four factors I think.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:26.730
Albedo, displacements, subsurface, and then
dynamic changes and deformations and so on.”

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.120
Albedo is the base color map for your character.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.920
Imagine the color of a face in a void.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.710
No features, no wrinkles, no lights shining
on it.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:41.070
See how I make the cheeks a little red, the
forehead a little lighter.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.750
That base is crucial to realism, and it's
incredibly dynamic.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:51.510
Based on Rosa Salazar's real skin, they adjusted
Alita’s albedo map for different moods,

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.960
health, everything.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:59.180
“So we could then sort of compare the albedo
map when she's like really angry, for example,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.261
with a neutral pose, and then extract basically
like a blood flow map from that.

00:03:03.261 --> 00:03:08.980
And the shader could then, when she gets angry
in her performance, dial in that extra blood

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.030
flow.”

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.060
Displacement maps push the scan up or down.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:19.680
Imagine how this little guy is flat, but features
or a wrinkle here might change the height of

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.680
his face.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:26.920
Look at all the detail in the Scorpion King’s
face here versus Imhotep’s face in the next

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:27.990
shot.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.090
That's the detail you can see in the close
ups in Alita.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.610
It's way smaller than wrinkles.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.470
“We call it micro geometry, even pore level
displacement that's what gives you your oiliness,

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.370
the specular response in your skin, which
you can see even just looking at me in the

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.900
in the camera here, you know, my forehead
is very different to my nose, very different

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.900
to my cheeks, and to my chin.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.480
Unfortunately, my forehead is quite shiny,
my nose also shiny, but my cheeks less so

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.130
and that they're fairly isotropic, without
direction, I have a very clear flow direction

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.200
that way, a flow direction this way.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.880
We have a sort of reverse flow direction around
my chin.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.100
So we actually draw curves on along these
flow lines.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.180
Some extreme close ups, we you know, we knew
that the camera was going to basically fly

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.270
into Alita’s eye.”

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:16.669
But the difference between this face and this
one isn't just the skin.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:18.560
It's what happens beneath it.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.780
“My name is Henrik Wann Jensen, and I'm
the chief scientist of Luxion, makers of Keyshot.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.060
And I specialize in computer graphics, in
answering the question why do things look

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.850
the way they do and how can you simulate it
on a computer?

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.560
See when we wrote the first paper as you may
have noticed, we did some measurements in

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.560
it.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.130
So we actually went to the local supermarket
with a laser pointer.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.680
I was shining on on the on the milk, we were
shining on the meat.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.610
And actually the guys who ran the supermarket
came to us and what are you doing?

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.100
This is not good.”

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:58.710
The big idea of that breakthrough 2001 paper,
work that got the team to the technical Oscars,

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.840
was that the way they'd seen light bounce
through food was true of almost everything.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.120
And if it could be simulated more quickly,
that would make all computer graphics look

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.210
more real, including skin.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.050
See how the laser bounces off the spoon?

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.230
But when I put my hand in front of it, the
light passes through, and even bounces around

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:20.440
underneath?

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:26.550
This drawing breaks down as an illustration,
because light passes through and bounces under

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.690
our skin.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.830
Computers could simulate subsurface scattering,
like they do with this marble.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:36.340
But they did it by simulating every single
bouncing light photon.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.340
That took way too long.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.870
So most of the time, computer animation couldn't
bother with it.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:47.130
It makes the Rock look wrong — light is
bouncing off him, not passing through the

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.130
skin.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.130
It affected other shots, too.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.800
“In Shrek, they actually have a cookie guy
and some milk.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.960
And if you ever see that shot, it looks like
white paint, which was exactly the example

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.440
we said if you don't do this properly, it's
actually going to look like white paint.”

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.720
Jensen and his colleagues figured out how
to simulate subsurface scattering more efficiently.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.080
That changed animation.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.630
“Then in Shrek 2, they now knew about this
technology.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.830
And then they went all in on the milk.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:14.900
So they had a ton of milk.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.900
But they now did the full subsurface scattering.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.910
And so it had a little bit of an impact on
that as well.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:27.480
it's funny because I went to talk to Sony
and you never know what people are interested

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.480
in.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.880
They say, oh, you're the milk guy.”

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.930
Subsurface scattering could efficiently simulate
the different ways light passed through whole

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:38.980
versus skim milk, and also skin, or at least
the beginning of it.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:43.210
“But when we created the initial subsurface
scattering algorithm, you're set up based

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:47.710
on this assumption that you hit the skin and
then everything is the same underneath.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.389
And of course, real human skin is not like
that.

00:06:50.389 --> 00:06:53.050
We have different layers like epidermis, dermis.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:57.110
And we looked at sort of the basic things
that decide what humans can look like.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:00.540
And it turns out melanin, you actually have
different melanin types, we have one that's

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:05.050
lighter melanin type, one that's more on the
brown side, on the dark side, you have to

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.330
have mixtures of those to get the correct
skins.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:12.300
You could actually do a very convincing, now,
rendering of human skin.”

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:19.921
“So, you know, I've talked about how we
define regions for micro geometry, some of

00:07:19.921 --> 00:07:23.100
the things we did also revolved around dynamic
scans.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:30.400
So we actually had footage of what scan data,
so it's this 3d three dimensional data of

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.840
Rosa running through Harvard lines of sort
of every expression we could we could ask

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.260
her to do.”

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.830
Harvard lines are a series of sentences that
help hit all the phonemes—chunks of speech

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:44.050
in a conversation.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:49.970
It's for audio, but it can help artists see
every possible way somebody's mouth can move

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:51.300
when they're talking.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.300
I'm doing this video.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:59.575
It includes these things called Harvard lines,
and I was hoping you could read some of them.

00:07:59.575 --> 00:08:08.413
[Reading overlapping Harvard lines]

00:08:08.413 --> 00:08:10.626
Early reviews for Final Fantasy: The Spirits

00:08:10.626 --> 00:08:14.670
Within focused on it as a treat for the eyeballs.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:20.070
Since then, increasingly complex simulations
of not just skin but light and movement have

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.740
made visual effects look even more real.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.930
The only question is how much further is left
to go.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:32.810
“Take the winding path to reach the lake,
note closely the size of the gas tank.

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:39.020
It snowed rained and hailed the same morning
the meal was cooked before the bell rang.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:42.618
What joy there is in living.”

00:08:45.843 --> 00:08:49.270
“One of the immediate effects that that
sort of the visual effects industry they jumped

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.670
on right away was if you also have someone
who's lit from behind, you see light passing

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:54.700
through the ear.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:59.300
If you look at movies like like Harry Potter
with Dobby, the character, they were the first

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.150
to really adopt this technology.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:04.710
They all had big ears so they were very excited
about that, and you'll see a lot of glowing

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.290
light coming through the ears.”

